{"abstain":false,"answer":"In the Electoral College, a candidate must receive an absolute majority of electoral votes—270 out of 538—to win the presidency. [S1][S2]

If no presidential candidate receives a majority, the Twelfth Amendment requires the House of Representatives to meet immediately and choose the president from the top three presidential electoral-vote winners. [S1][S2]

If no vice-presidential candidate receives a majority, the Senate chooses the vice president from the top two vice-presidential electoral-vote winners. [S2]","citations":["S1","S2"]}
